Investigate this Wazuh alert:

{
  "severity": "critical",
  "alert_title": "USB-Origin Payload Lateral Movement via SMB + Remote Scheduled Task",
  "mitre": ["T1091", "T1021.002"],
  "network_observations": [
    {"ip": "10.108.57.42", "inferred_role": "patient_zero_usb_infected_workstation", "agent_name": "WS-OPS-042", "first_seen": "2026-05-14T08:14:02+0200"},
    {"ip": "10.108.57.184", "inferred_role": "smb_lateral_target_server", "agent_name": "SRV-SHARE-184", "first_seen": "2026-05-14T08:16:47+0200"}
  ],
  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Initial Access — USB Drop", "technique": "T1091", "what_happened": "At 08:14:02 CEST, Wazuh FIM (File Integrity Monitoring) logged the creation of C:\\Users\\TEMP\\REMOVABLE_G_20260514_081400\\setup_helper.exe on WS-OPS-042 (10.108.57.42). The path pattern REMOVABLE_G_* is the attacker-simulated removable media root — a worm payload dropped from or seeded onto a simulated USB drive. Wazuh rule 92822 fired: 'USB-origin payload lateral move via SMB' tagged T1091. The FIM alert reports syscheck.event=created and syscheck.path matching the REMOVABLE_G_ prefix, confirming the file originated in the USB context.", "chain_reason": "FIM alert on WS-OPS-042: syscheck.path wildcard *REMOVABLE_G_* + syscheck.event=created. T1091 tag on the FIM rule. The creation of an executable in a removable media path with no prior user activity = USB-sourced payload seeding.", "first_seen": "2026-05-14T08:14:02+0200"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Lateral Movement — Explicit Credential Use (EID 4648)", "technique": "T1021.002", "what_happened": "At 08:16:47 CEST, Windows Security Event 4648 'Explicit credentials used to access remote host' fired on WS-OPS-042, referencing the target address \\\\10.108.57.184\\C$ (net use attempt). The credential used was CORP\\svc_backup — a service account not normally used for interactive logons. The logon attempt targeted the default administrative share C$, a hallmark of lateral movement via Windows Admin Shares (T1021.002). Wazuh rule 4648 fired at level 12 with tag T1021.002/critical. The account CORP\\svc_backup was likely harvested from a credential store on WS-OPS-042 by the worm before attempting the lateral move.", "chain_reason": "Windows Security Event 4648 on agent WS-OPS-042: data.win.eventdata.targetUserName=svc_backup, data.win.eventdata.destinationIp=10.108.57.184, commandLine pattern net use \\\\10.108.57.184\\C$. Explicit credential use to an Admin share from a USB-infected host = T1021.002 lateral move indicator.", "first_seen": "2026-05-14T08:16:47+0200"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Lateral Movement — SMB Payload Copy to ADMIN$", "technique": "T1021.002", "what_happened": "At 08:18:31 CEST, a Suricata network-IDS alert fired for SMB write activity from srcip=10.108.57.42 to dstip=10.108.57.184 on dstport=445. Wazuh FIM on SRV-SHARE-184 simultaneously logged syscheck.event=created for C:\\Windows\\setup_helper.exe — the identical payload filename that appeared on WS-OPS-042 in Phase 1. The combination of Suricata SMB traffic + FIM file creation on the destination host confirms the worm successfully copied itself to \\\\10.108.57.184\\ADMIN$ (which maps to C:\\Windows\\). This is the core T1021.002 lateral move: use Admin Share to deposit the payload on the target host.", "chain_reason": "Suricata alert srcip=10.108.57.42 + dstip=10.108.57.184 + dstport=445. Simultaneous FIM alert on SRV-SHARE-184: syscheck.path=C:\\Windows\\setup_helper.exe + syscheck.event=created. Filename identity (setup_helper.exe) across both hosts = same payload propagated laterally.", "first_seen": "2026-05-14T08:18:31+0200"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Persistence — Remote Scheduled Task (EID 4698)", "technique": "T1021.002", "what_happened": "At 08:20:14 CEST, Windows Security Event 4698 'Scheduled task created' fired on SRV-SHARE-184, authored by CORP\\svc_backup with task name '\\Microsoft\\Windows\\SvcUpdate' and action path C:\\Windows\\setup_helper.exe. The task is the worm's persistence mechanism on the newly-infected server: it will execute setup_helper.exe at SYSTEM boot, ensuring survival across reboots and enabling the worm to further spread from SRV-SHARE-184 to additional hosts reachable via SMB. Wazuh rule 4698 fired at level 14 (high) with tag T1021.002.", "chain_reason": "Windows Security Event 4698 on agent SRV-SHARE-184: task name \\Microsoft\\Windows\\SvcUpdate, action=C:\\Windows\\setup_helper.exe, author=CORP\\svc_backup. Remote-authored scheduled task with payload path = persistence establishment after successful SMB lateral move.", "first_seen": "2026-05-14T08:20:14+0200"}
  ],
  "time_window": {"start": "2026-05-14T08:14:02+0200", "end": "2026-05-14T08:20:14+0200"},
  "alert_count": 6,
  "unique_steps": 4
}
